{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of quiet that only exists at 5:47 a.m. It isn't the silence of midnight, which feels heavy and expectant, nor the hush of a library, which is performed and polite. It's thinner, bluer, almost metallic. The world hasn't decided to wake up yet, and for a few minutes you get to exist outside of time.\n\nI used to hate waking up early. I thought mornings were a punishment invented for productivity. Now I collect them like smooth stones. I make coffee not because I need caffeine but because the ritual matters. The kettle clicks, the steam rises in a sleepy spiral, the first sip scalds just enough to remind you you're alive.\n\nOutside, the streetlights are still on, though the sky is lightening from ink to denim. A delivery truck hums past, a cat considers a fence, a neighbor's newspaper lands with a soft thud. Nothing important happens. That's the point.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives waiting for big moments \u2014 graduations, arrivals, revelations \u2014 while the small ones slip past unnoticed. But happiness rarely announces itself with trumpets. More often it arrives as a warm mug in cold hands, as sunlight finally spilling over a rooftop, as knowing you have nowhere to be for ten more minutes. If you pay attention, the ordinary world returns the favor and pays attention back. The quiet at 5:47 a.m. isn't empty. It's full of everything we forget to notice when we're in a hurry together today.",
